  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "9eXWGZSkUsIquYDE9C+w9NV+Qkw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:11:48.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Positioning articulated by Wouter across both meetings:\n\nMany industry tools already handle **low-level, specific data** well (3D geometry, quantities, schedules). What relies on manual, fragmented processes is **high-level business intelligence and document-heavy project management** \u2014 the layer of requirements documents, approvals, onboarding, risk registers, cost overviews (BAI weekly, 00:06:42).\n\nThe construction industry amplifies the opportunity: \"nomad\" teams that re-form per project, plus standardized requirements like BIM execution plans, mean **reusable document components** can standardize workflows across projects **without locking clients into a single proprietary platform** (00:09:54).\n\nSupporting properties:\n- **Local-first technology** emphasizes data protection \u2014 a direct fit for the sector's data-sovereignty constraints (EU-hosting requirements that prohibit some SaaS tools) and its rigid, heterogeneous stakeholder stacks (02:04:17)\n- **Structured workflows, human-in-the-loop approvals, attributable history** address the siloed-data problem that limits AI effectiveness (00:01:06)\n- Role: transform unstructured data into structured data to support business intelligence, reducing reliance on tools with excessive licensing costs (02:02:55)",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T18:11:48.457286+00:00"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:11:48.000Z"
    }
  },
